## Purpose

The `JVP_KIND` macro is an internal helper used within the `jv` implementation of the `jq` JSON processor. It extracts the *kind* field from a `jv` value's internal flag word.  

Every JSON value in `jv` is represented by a `jv` struct, whose flags encode metadata such as the value's category (null, boolean, number, string, array, object, etc.). `JVP_KIND` masks those flags with `KIND_MASK` to retrieve just the category bits. This macro is primarily used by other internal `jv` routines to dispatch on the type of a value.

Because it is a simple bitwise operation, its complexity is constant time, `O(1)`.

## Signature

```c
#define JVP_KIND(j)   (JVP_FLAGS(j) & KIND_MASK)
```

The symbol is a function-like macro defined in `src/jv.c`.

## Parameters

| Parameter | Description |
|-----------|-------------|
| `j`       | A `jv` value from which the kind bits are to be extracted. The value is evaluated exactly once. |

No other parameters are required.

## Returns

`JVP_KIND` returns an integer value representing the kind or type of the `jv` value. The returned value is typically compared against the `JV_KIND_*` constants (e.g., `JV_KIND_NULL`, `JV_KIND_NUMBER`, `JV_KIND_STRING`, `JV_KIND_ARRAY`, `JV_KIND_OBJECT`, etc.) defined by the `jv` system.

## Example Usage

Because `JVP_KIND` is an internal macro, it is usually seen within the `jv` source itself. A hypothetical dispatch function might look like:

```c
#include "jv.h"

/* Hypothetical internal function - JVP_KIND is not part of the public API */
const char *describe_kind(jv value) {
    switch (JVP_KIND(value)) {
        case JV_KIND_NULL:    return "null";
        case JV_KIND_FALSE:   return "false";
        case JV_KIND_TRUE:    return "true";
        case JV_KIND_NUMBER:  return "number";
        case JV_KIND_STRING:  return "string";
        case JV_KIND_ARRAY:   return "array";
        case JV_KIND_OBJECT:  return "object";
        default:              return "unknown";
    }
}
```

In practice, the macro is used throughout `src/jv.c` to branch on the type of a `jv` value:

```c
jv jv_copy(jv j) {
    if (JVP_KIND(j) == JV_KIND_STRING) {
        /* Copy the underlying string data */
    }
    return j;
}
```

Note that `JVP_KIND` is not part of the public `jv` API; it is an implementation detail exposed only within the library's internal compilation units.

